  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 125 AM EDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nSurface high pressure is shifting east underneath mid level ridging \nand this keeps the area dry once again. One thing that does happen, \nthough, is that warm advection commences bringing a return to upper \ncloudiness for this evening and tonight. This warm advection also \nhelps southern areas get into the upper 70s to around 80 degree \nhighs for Wednesday. This is especially the case because the \nvorticity is more focused to our north and west as opposed to \noverhead. The best of the instability (still less than 100 \nJ/kg of MUCAPE) finally arrives around 00z in our northwest, but \nsurface dew points are still below 60F and the best forcing has \nmoved more northeastward instead of eastward into southwest Lower \nMI. All of this to say that it is possible that rain gets into the \narea Wednesday afternoon/evening, but severe weather is expected to \nbe limited and thunderstorm chances are low, if not non-existent. \nThis is why we have good chances to see those 80 degree highs on \nWednesday is that, despite cloud cover, we have limited rain chances \ndown south there. By the time we get to Thursday, the stalled front \nto our west slides eastward slowly as a trough moves east-\nsoutheastward across southern Canada. Later Thursday and Thursday \nnight is our best thunderstorm chance of this rainy period as the \nNAM brings through areas of instability up to 1000 J/kg of MUCAPE. \n\nRain may linger into Friday morning, otherwise, the trend will be to \ndry out Friday afternoon as a surface high pressure system moves in. \nThis dry period also looks to last a while as the later \nSaturday/Saturday night rain chance doesn't have much model \nagreement at this point. So, a series of high pressure systems move \nthrough and the area remains underneath an area of subsidence as a \nresult.\n\nHighs in the 70s to around 80 today through Thursday, trend lower, \nbehind the cold front Thursday night, allowing for a return to highs \nin the 60s and lows in the 40s for Friday through early next\nweek.",
